# Coding Rules

All code must follow the project's requirements, architecture, and documentation.

Before implementing code, read the relevant documentation and existing implementation.

---

# 1. Core Principles

- Write production-ready code.
- Prioritize readability and maintainability.
- Prefer simple solutions over unnecessary abstractions.
- Follow existing project conventions.
- Reuse existing utilities and components where appropriate.
- Avoid duplicated logic.
- Keep responsibilities clear.
- Keep functions focused.
- Do not introduce unnecessary dependencies.
- Do not overengineer.

---

# 2. TypeScript

Use TypeScript for application code.

TypeScript strictness must be preserved.

Never use:

```ts
any;
```

Do not use `any` to suppress type errors.

Do not weaken compiler settings to make code compile.

Prefer explicit types when they improve readability.

Prefer type inference when the type is obvious.

---

# 3. Interface vs Type

Use `interface` for object-shaped contracts that may benefit from extension or declaration merging.

Example:

```ts
interface User {
  id: string;
  name: string;
}
```

Use `type` for:

- Union types
- Intersection types
- Tuple types
- Function types
- Mapped types
- Conditional types
- Other type-level compositions

Example:

```ts
type TaskStatus = "backlog" | "todo" | "in_progress" | "review" | "done";
```

Do not blindly use `interface` for every type.

Choose the construct that best expresses the intended type.

---

# 4. Type Safety

Prefer type-safe implementations.

Avoid unsafe type assertions:

```ts
value as SomeType;
```

unless the assertion is justified and there is no safer alternative.

Do not use:

```ts
as any
```

Do not use:

```ts
// @ts-ignore
```

unless there is an exceptional, documented reason.

Prefer:

- Type guards
- Zod validation
- Generics
- Discriminated unions
- Properly typed function parameters
- Properly typed return values

---

# 5. Null and Undefined

Handle nullable values explicitly.

Do not assume that optional data exists.

Avoid unsafe code such as:

```ts
user!.name;
```

unless the invariant is guaranteed and clearly established.

Prefer explicit handling:

```ts
if (!user) {
  return;
}
```

or appropriate fallback behavior.

---

# 6. Async / Await

Prefer `async/await` for asynchronous code.

Example:

```ts
const project = await getProject(projectId);
```

Avoid unnecessary Promise chains:

```ts
getProject(projectId)
  .then(...)
  .catch(...)
```

Use `Promise.all()` when independent asynchronous operations can safely execute in parallel.

Example:

```ts
const [projects, notifications] = await Promise.all([
  getProjects(),
  getNotifications(),
]);
```

Do not use parallel execution when operations depend on each other.

---

# 7. Error Handling

Do not silently ignore errors.

Avoid:

```ts
try {
  await doSomething();
} catch {}
```

Handle errors intentionally.

Use appropriate error boundaries between:

- UI
- API
- Service
- Repository

Do not expose internal implementation details to users.

---

# 8. Functions

Keep functions small and focused.

A function should generally have one clear responsibility.

Avoid functions that:

- Fetch data
- Validate data
- Transform data
- Update the database
- Format UI output

all at once.

Split responsibilities when a function becomes difficult to understand or test.

---

# 9. File Size

Prefer keeping files under approximately 300 lines.

The 300-line guideline is not absolute.

Do not split a file into many meaningless files just to satisfy the line count.

Split files when separation improves:

- Readability
- Testability
- Reusability
- Responsibility boundaries

---

# 10. Naming

Use descriptive names.

Prefer:

```ts
getProjectById();
createProject();
updateTaskStatus();
calculateProjectProgress();
```

over:

```ts
getData();
create();
update();
calc();
```

Avoid unnecessary abbreviations.

---

# 11. Variables

Use `const` by default.

Use `let` only when reassignment is required.

Avoid `var`.

Prefer immutable data where practical.

---

# 12. Boolean Naming

Boolean variables should clearly communicate their meaning.

Prefer:

```ts
isLoading;
isAuthenticated;
isArchived;
hasPermission;
canEdit;
```

Avoid:

```ts
loading;
auth;
archive;
permission;
```

when the boolean meaning is unclear.

---

# 13. Constants

Use constants for values that have semantic meaning or are reused.

Example:

```ts
const MAX_PROJECTS_PER_PAGE = 50;
```

Avoid unexplained magic numbers.

Bad:

```ts
if (projects.length > 50) {
```

Better:

```ts
if (projects.length > MAX_PROJECTS_PER_PAGE) {
```

---

# 14. Comments

Prefer self-explanatory code.

Do not write comments that merely describe obvious code.

Avoid:

```ts
// Get project
const project = await getProject(id);
```

Write comments when they explain:

- Why something is necessary
- Why an unusual implementation was chosen
- Important constraints
- Non-obvious business rules
- Workarounds

Example:

```ts
// Keep the initial login request database-free so the public login page
// remains fast even when the application database has not been accessed recently.
```

---

# 15. React

Follow React best practices.

Prefer functional components.

Do not use class components unless explicitly required.

Keep components focused.

Avoid excessively large components.

If a component becomes difficult to understand, split it by responsibility.

---

# 16. React Props

Define explicit props.

Example:

```ts
interface TaskCardProps {
  task: Task;
  onOpen: (taskId: string) => void;
}
```

Avoid overly generic props such as:

```ts
data: any;
```

Never use `any`.

---

# 17. React State

Do not create state when derived values can be calculated.

Avoid:

```ts
const [completedCount, setCompletedCount] = useState(0);
```

when it can safely be derived from existing data.

Prefer:

```ts
const completedCount = tasks.filter((task) => task.status === "done").length;
```

Avoid unnecessary global state.

Keep local state local whenever possible.

---

# 18. useEffect

Do not use `useEffect` as a default mechanism for application logic.

Before using `useEffect`, determine whether the logic can be handled by:

- Server Components
- Event handlers
- Derived state
- Server-side data fetching
- TanStack Query

Use `useEffect` when synchronization with an external system or browser API actually requires it.

---

# 19. Server / Client Boundary

Follow `docs/04_architecture.md`.

Server Components are the default.

Do not add:

```tsx
"use client";
```

unless the component requires client-side behavior.

Keep Client Components as small as practical.

Do not make an entire page a Client Component because one child component requires interactivity.

---

# 20. Data Fetching

Do not fetch data unnecessarily.

Prefer server-side data fetching for initial page data when appropriate.

Use TanStack Query for client-side server state when its features are actually needed.

Avoid duplicate requests.

Avoid fetching the same data independently in multiple components when it can be shared appropriately.

---

# 21. API Calls

Do not put API request logic directly into many unrelated components.

Centralize repeated API communication when appropriate.

Validate API responses when required by the architecture.

Handle:

- Loading
- Success
- Empty
- Error

states explicitly.

---

# 22. Validation

All external input must be validated.

Use Zod for:

- Forms
- API requests
- Query parameters
- URL parameters
- User-provided data

Client-side validation does not replace server-side validation.

---

# 23. Business Logic

Do not put business logic inside UI components.

Business logic belongs in the appropriate service or domain layer.

Bad:

```tsx
const progress = (completedTasks.length / tasks.length) * 100;
```

when the calculation is a shared business rule used across multiple parts of the application.

Prefer a reusable domain/service function:

```ts
calculateProjectProgress(tasks);
```

Do not create a service for trivial presentation logic.

Use judgment.

---

# 24. Database Access

Do not access the database directly from UI components.

Do not import Drizzle or database clients into Client Components.

Database access belongs to the repository/data-access layer.

Follow:

```text
UI
 ↓
API / Server Action
 ↓
Validation
 ↓
Service
 ↓
Repository
 ↓
Drizzle
 ↓
D1
```

---

# 25. Security

Treat all external input as untrusted.

Never trust:

- Client-provided user IDs
- Workspace IDs
- Project IDs
- Permission flags
- Role information

Authorization must be verified on the server.

Never expose secrets in client-side code.

Never commit secrets.

---

# 26. Reusability

Write reusable code when reuse provides real value.

Do not abstract code prematurely.

Avoid creating a generic utility solely because two pieces of code look similar.

Extract shared logic when:

- It is used by multiple features.
- The behavior has a clear common meaning.
- The abstraction improves maintainability.

---

# 27. Duplication

Avoid duplicated business logic.

Before creating new functionality:

1. Search the repository.
2. Check existing utilities.
3. Check existing services.
4. Check existing hooks.
5. Check existing components.

Reuse existing functionality when appropriate.

Do not duplicate functionality simply because it is faster.

---

# 28. Imports

Keep imports clean.

Prefer project aliases where configured.

Example:

```ts
import { Button } from "@/components/ui/button";
```

Avoid unnecessary relative paths across distant directories.

Remove unused imports.

Do not leave commented-out imports.

---

# 29. Dependency Management

Do not install a dependency without a clear reason.

Before installing a package:

1. Check whether the functionality already exists.
2. Check existing project dependencies.
3. Consider bundle size.
4. Consider maintenance.
5. Consider security.
6. Consider Cloudflare compatibility.

Do not add a dependency for a trivial piece of functionality.

---

# 30. ESLint

Code must pass ESLint.

Before completing a task:

```bash
pnpm lint
```

must pass.

Do not disable ESLint rules merely to make code pass.

If an ESLint rule must be disabled, the reason must be clear and localized.

Avoid broad ESLint disable comments.

---

# 31. Prettier

Follow the project's Prettier configuration.

Do not manually introduce formatting styles that conflict with Prettier.

Do not disable formatting rules unnecessarily.

Format changed files before completing the task.

---

# 32. Type Checking

Before completing a task, run the project's TypeScript type check.

Example:

```bash
pnpm typecheck
```

If the project uses a different command, follow `package.json`.

Do not ignore TypeScript errors.

Do not use `any` to resolve them.

---

# 33. Testing

New functionality must include appropriate tests.

Tests belong under:

```text
tests/
├── unit/
├── integration/
└── e2e/
```

Use:

- Unit tests for isolated logic.
- Integration tests for interactions between systems.
- E2E tests for important user journeys.

Do not write tests that merely reproduce implementation details.

Test behavior.

---

# 34. Testability

Write code that can be tested independently.

Prefer:

```text
Pure Function
 ↓
Unit Test
```

and:

```text
Service
 ↓
Repository
 ↓
Integration Test
```

Avoid tightly coupling business logic to React components.

---

# 35. Git

Do not modify unrelated files.

Keep changes focused.

Do not generate temporary files.

Do not commit:

```text
.env
.env.local
secrets
credentials
build artifacts
```

Follow `.gitignore`.

---

# 36. Refactoring

Do not perform large unrelated refactors while implementing a feature.

If refactoring is necessary:

1. Explain why.
2. Keep the refactor focused.
3. Preserve existing behavior.
4. Run relevant tests.

Do not rewrite working code merely because another style is preferred.

---

# 37. Existing Code

Before creating new code, inspect the existing implementation.

Do not assume that a feature or utility does not already exist.

Prefer extending existing code over creating competing implementations.

Respect established conventions unless there is a clear reason to change them.

---

# 38. Documentation

When code changes the documented behavior, update the relevant documentation.

Relevant documents include:

```text
docs/01_requirements.md
docs/02_basic-design.md
docs/03_detail-design/
docs/04_architecture.md
docs/05_database.md
docs/06_api.md
docs/07_component_design.md
docs/08_ui-guideline.md
docs/product.md
docs/roadmap.md
docs/screen-list.md
docs/development-log.md
```

Do not leave documentation inconsistent with implementation.

---

# 39. Implementation Checklist

Before completing implementation, verify:

- [ ] TypeScript passes
- [ ] ESLint passes
- [ ] Prettier passes
- [ ] No `any`
- [ ] No unnecessary type assertions
- [ ] No unnecessary dependencies
- [ ] No duplicated business logic
- [ ] Existing components/utilities were checked
- [ ] Server / Client boundary is appropriate
- [ ] Validation is implemented
- [ ] Authorization is implemented where required
- [ ] Tests are added or updated
- [ ] Loading state is handled
- [ ] Empty state is handled
- [ ] Error state is handled
- [ ] Documentation is updated if necessary

---

# 40. Final Coding Principles

Always prefer:

```text
Readable
        over
Clever

Simple
        over
Over-engineered

Type-safe
        over
Convenient

Reusable
        over
Duplicated

Tested
        over
Assumed

Explicit
        over
Implicit

Maintainable
        over
Short
```

The goal is not to produce the smallest amount of code.

The goal is to produce code that another engineer can understand, test, modify, and safely maintain.
